# Torn page DOM

Torn is a jQuery-and-React hybrid that re-renders constantly, so almost everything here exists to avoid racing it.
Reuse these helpers instead of touching the DOM directly — an equivalent almost certainly already exists.

## Finding elements

`document.querySelector`, `querySelectorAll`, `getElementById` and `getElementsByTagName` are **banned by Oxlint**. Use
`@common/utils/functions/find-elements`:

```ts
import { findAllElements, findElement } from "@common/utils/functions/find-elements";

const wrapper = findElement(".content-wrapper");                 // throws ElementNotFoundError if missing
const maybe = findElement(".optional-thing", true);              // returns null instead
const scoped = findElement("[class*='title_']", parent, true);   // scoped + optional
const rows = findAllElements("ul.item-list > li");               // real array, not a NodeList
```

The throwing default is intentional: a feature that can't find its anchor should fail loudly and get reported, not
silently do nothing. Pass `true` only where absence is genuinely expected. `findElementWithText(tag, text, optional?)`
covers the XPath text-match case.

## Waiting for content

Never poll with `setInterval` or guess with `setTimeout`. `@common/utils/functions/requires` wraps a shared
MutationObserver per parent node:

| Helper | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| `requireElement(selector, { parent?, timeout?, invert? })` | Wait for one element (5s default timeout). |
| `requireCondition(fn, { delay?, maxCycles? })` | Wait for arbitrary state that isn't a selector. |
| `observeChain(root, [sel1, sel2], onReached)` | Multi-stage React rendering, where children appear after parents. |
| `requireContent()` / `requireSidebar()` | The two most common page anchors. |
| `requireItemsLoaded()` / `requireChatsLoaded()` | Lazily-rendered lists. |
| `requireDOMContentLoaded()` / `requireDOMInteractive()` | Document lifecycle. |

Prefer reacting to Torn's own traffic over observing the DOM when you can:
`addFetchListener` / `addXHRListener` from `@common/utils/functions/listeners`, and the `EVENT_CHANNELS` custom events
that `src/common/pages/*-page.ts` modules publish. A raw `MutationObserver` is a last resort for genuinely continuous
churn — scope it as tightly as possible.

## Building markup

`elementBuilder` from `@common/utils/functions/dom` instead of manual `createElement` chains:

```ts
import { elementBuilder } from "@common/utils/functions/dom";

const row = elementBuilder({
	type: "div",
	class: "tt-row",
	children: [elementBuilder({ type: "span", text: formatNumber(value) })],
	events: { click: () => open() },
});
```

HTML written as a template string inside a `.ts` file is not auto-formatted — indent it by hand if you must use one.

## Containers

A container is the standard TornTools panel on a Torn page: titled header, optional collapse whose state persists to
`filters.containers[id]`, and themed chrome. Use it for anything panel-shaped rather than inventing a wrapper.

```ts
import { createContainer } from "@common/utils/functions/containers";

const { content } = createContainer("My Feature", { nextElement: findElement(".content-title") });
```

Position with exactly one of `parentElement`, `nextElement`, `previousElement` or `defaultPosition: true`. The `id`
defaults to the camelCased title, which is also the persistence key — changing a title silently resets users' collapse
state.

For filter panels use `createFilter()` from `@common/utils/functions/filters` with its section builders
(`checkboxSection`, `sliderSection`, ...); it creates the container for you and handles persistence and presets.

## Reusable widgets

`src/common/utils/elements/` holds the input widgets, all returning `{ element, ..., dispose() }`. Check here before
writing a control: `checkbox`, `checkbox-duo` (yes/no/both), `checkbox-list`, `radio`, `radio-list`, `select` and
`createMultiSelect`, `textbox`, `slider` (`DualRangeSlider`), `table`. Call `dispose()` when tearing down.

## Torn's own markup

- Torn's CSS classes are fine as **selectors** but must never be reused as styling hooks — they change without notice.
- Hashed class names are rebuilt on every Torn deploy. Match the stable prefix with an attribute selector:
  `[class*='SOMENAME_']`, never `.SOMENAME_xyz`.
- Prefix your own classes and IDs with `tt-` so they're identifiable and can't collide.
- Colours come from the `--tt-*` variables in `src/common/utils/global/globalVariables.css` (`--tt-color-green`,
  `--tt-color-red`, `--tt-background-popup`, ...). They already handle Torn's light/dark modes via `body.dark-mode`.
  Add a variable there rather than hard-coding a hex value in a feature stylesheet.
- Torn's React inputs ignore direct `.value` assignment. Use `updateReactInput()` / `updateReactCheckbox()` from
  `@common/utils/functions/torn`.
- Tailwind is **not** available in content scripts. Write plain CSS in the feature's stylesheet.

## Page scripts vs. content scripts

Content scripts run in an isolated world and cannot see page globals like `torn.model` or jQuery. Reading those requires
an injected script — see the extension shell rule for the `--inject` pattern. In userscripts, use
`RUNTIME_INFORMATION.getWindow()` rather than `window`.
